{#
  Service for a k8s-hosted service — the single-port ClusterIP Service every plain web-app role
  ships.

  NO PLAIN ROLE CALLS THIS MACRO. Plain roles ship no Service template at all:
  `k8s/manifests` renders `ansible/templates/service-default.yaml.j2` for them,
  which is the one caller of the plain form. That file reads the name, the port, and
  the optional `service_port_name` / `service_extra_ports` off the role's containers_list entry.
  To change what a plain role's Service looks like, change that file, not a role.

  The macro's other callers are the roles whose Service needs something the entry cannot say:
  authelia (redis), freshrss, game-stats, karakeep, n8n, nut, nut-exporter, pihole-exporter,
  prowlarr (flaresolverr) and tdarr each write a Service template around it. Most of them render
  a second Service, a fixed port, or a comment the entry has nowhere to carry. observability
  calls it from three of its workload files (grafana, kube-state-metrics, otel-collector) with
  `namespace=`.

  A Service that does NOT fit the macro at all, and why each stays hand-written — check these
  before filing
  "role X still inlines a Service", because each one was converted and reverted or measured
  and refused:

    selector ≠ name   The macro sets `selector.app` to the Service NAME. traefik's
                      metrics-service, freshrss's `feed-cache` (pod label
                      `freshrss-feed-cache`) and qbittorrent's service-wireguard all point a
                      Service at a differently-labelled pod, so none can use it.
    port ≠ targetPort qbittorrent's service.yaml.j2 publishes `container_item.port` and
                      targets `qbittorrent_k8s_webui_port`. This macro emits one number for
                      both, which is why the `port` argument above says there is only one.
    extra metadata    registry's Service pins a `clusterIP`. observability's loki, tempo and
                      prometheus Services pin a `clusterIP` too (host-side query tools carry
                      the address), and prometheus carries a `headlamp-prometheus` label. The
                      macro emits neither.
    no selector AT ALL  deploy-ui's Service is selector-less on purpose — its backend is a host
                      process reached through a hand-written EndpointSlice, not a pod.
    LoadBalancer      the macro is ClusterIP-only (it emits no `type:`), which rules out
                      jellyfin's service-lan, mosquitto, pihole, valheim, terraria, traefik and
                      wg-easy.

  Arguments mirror ingressroute()'s style:
    name       Service (and selector `app` label) name; matches the Deployment's own label and
               the IngressRoute this Service backs.
    port       Service and target port — this fleet has no services publishing on a different
               container port than their Service port, so there is only the one number.
    port_name  Set when a caller's Service needs a NAMED port rather than the bare port list
               every other role uses. Omit to get the plain form. home-assistant, homelab-mcp
               and zigbee2mqtt need one and reach it through `service_port_name` on their
               containers_list entry; nut-exporter and pihole-exporter pass it directly.
    extra_ports  Additional ports as [{name, port}] — a sidecar's, typically. sonarr, radarr
               and prowlarr carry the exportarr port this way, through `service_extra_ports`
               on their containers_list entry. Kubernetes REJECTS
               a Service that mixes named and unnamed ports, and it rejects it at apply time,
               after every repo-side check has read green. So passing extra_ports forces the
               primary port to be named too (`http` unless port_name says otherwise) rather than
               leaving that as something each caller has to remember.

    namespace  Namespace the Service lands in. Omit for `k8s_namespace`, which every caller
               but observability wants; observability passes `k8s_observability_namespace`,
               the same way it calls pvc().

  Call with a body to insert a comment block between `ports:` and the first `- port:` entry;
  call plain (no `{% call %}`) when there's nothing to say there. tdarr is the one caller: its
  list carries "The web UI only. tdarr_k8s_server_port is deliberately absent" on the line the
  fact governs. pvc()'s slot exists for the same reason, but it cannot reach a
  `storageClassName` line, so pi-peer-backup and valheim keep that reasoning beside their
  `*_storage_class` defaults instead; this slot does reach tdarr's line.
#}
{% macro service(name, port, port_name=none, extra_ports=[], namespace=none) -%}
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: {{ name }}
  namespace: {{ namespace | default(k8s_namespace, true) }}
spec:
  selector:
    app: {{ name }}
  ports:
{% if caller is defined %}{{ caller() }}{% endif %}
{% if port_name or extra_ports %}
    - name: {{ port_name | default('http', true) }}
      port: {{ port }}
      targetPort: {{ port }}
{% else %}
    - port: {{ port }}
      targetPort: {{ port }}
{% endif %}
{% for extra in extra_ports %}
    - name: {{ extra.name }}
      port: {{ extra.port }}
      targetPort: {{ extra.port }}
{% endfor %}
{%- endmacro %}
